WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.920
It may be hard to believe

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.800
that products based on
racial stereotypes survived

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.813
well into the 21st century.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.070
There was always these
friends I had breakfast with

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.240
and they always would take out

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.254
their Aunt Jemima jug.

00:00:39.254 --> 00:00:40.810
I haven't really heard
of any other syrups,

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.790
so I usually just
gravitate towards that one.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.650
It just seems like a super exploitation

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.724
of a stereotype.

00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:49.673
What do we want?
Justice!

00:00:49.673 --> 00:00:52.450
And when do we want it?
Now!

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.840
It took widespread protests in 2020

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.003
to send some companies a wake-up call.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.980
But even as some of this
controversial branding

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.549
slowly disappears off store shelves,

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:08.100
there could be an even more insidious

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.450
and harder to fix form of racial bias

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.520
coded into the technologies
that have become ubiquitous

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.880
in everyday life.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.546
Start your day with natural freshness

00:01:24.546 --> 00:01:25.704
from Darlie.

00:01:25.704 --> 00:01:27.750
One of the most popular toothpaste brands

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.840
in East Asia, Southeast Asia,

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.337
is called Darlie Toothpaste.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.337
Darlie is owned by Colgate-Palmolive

00:01:36.337 --> 00:01:37.920
and a local partner,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.190
and has a long history,

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.690
goes back to, I think, 1930s.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.603
And it originally started
out as Darkie toothpaste.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.500
The packaging was pretty explicit.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.690
It had a picture of minstrel singer

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.470
in blackface on the package

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.520
with the big smile with white teeth.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.450
This came about at a time when movies

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.393
like "The Jazz Singer" with
Al Jolson were very popular.

00:02:08.498 --> 00:02:10.710
♪ Smile, Darkie, smile for me ♪

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.000
Darkie Toothpaste

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.030
gives you a cool, fresh, tingling taste.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.146
You see how this is working
in terms of marketing.

00:02:18.146 --> 00:02:22.106
There's nothing wrong
with wanting white teeth,

00:02:22.106 --> 00:02:24.310
but when you contrast that

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.493
with this jet-black person,

00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:28.854
the message is very clear.

00:02:28.854 --> 00:02:31.517
If you don't want to have dark teeth,

00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:33.277
you don't want anything dark,

00:02:33.277 --> 00:02:34.247
use Darkie.

00:02:34.247 --> 00:02:35.717
It will solve that problem.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.070
They changed the name
in English to Darlie,

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.380
so one letter gets rid of the problem.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.640
They also got rid of the packaging,

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.710
so it was no longer just a picture

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.020
of a man in blackface.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.080
On the box, it was somebody
who was in a top hat,

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.130
and there was a black and white contrast,

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:03.440
still getting the point across,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.780
like, look "black, white,"

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.360
but it wasn't blackface.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.906
That said, though,

00:03:07.906 --> 00:03:09.333
they didn't change the name in Chinese.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.250
The issue of having these kinds of brands

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.130
with these racist legacies,

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.730
it's a bit more complicated here in Asia

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.770
than it is in other parts of the world,

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.054
in part, just because

00:03:38.054 --> 00:03:41.963
of the different racial dynamics in Asia.

00:03:42.982 --> 00:03:45.040
You don't have a lot of Black people.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.190
And so, people just don't think about

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:49.710
these issues the same way.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.940
So there's less pressure domestically

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.313
to do anything about them.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.000
Meanwhile, across the world,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.100
the United States has its own version

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.530
of the Darlie story,

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.500
in a product familiar to many people

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:15.750
on their breakfast table.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:18.530
There were two Black families

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:19.780
in the town I grew up in.

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:24.270
And Aunt Jemima was literally

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.263
the only Black person
I knew most of my life.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:29.577
We used to use it a lot

00:04:29.577 --> 00:04:30.480
when we were kids

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:31.540
and making pancakes in the morning

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.260
and using the syrup too.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.160
I had good memories from my childhood,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.520
using it all the time.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.010
Well, I don't necessarily
think Aunt Jemima was racist.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:42.470
I felt like it was just a
brand that named it by that,

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.363
and nothing more than that really.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.300
At the time of Aunt
Jemima's inception, 1889,

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.870
this was something where it was marketed

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.363
to White folks.

00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:00.890
You have this very recent memory

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:02.310
back then of just slavery,

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:03.810
what it meant to people

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.720
and what subservience meant

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:08.300
in terms of not only a proposition

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.683
but a role in the American way.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:20.410
The gentleman who came up with the idea

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:25.410
for the Aunt Jemima motif
and brand, Chris Rutt,

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.920
he attended a minstrel show in Missouri

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.430
in the 1880s,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.764
where he saw a White man
in blackface performing

00:05:34.764 --> 00:05:37.270
a minstrel role as a mammy

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:40.390
to target and make fun
of African Americans,

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.650
but also African American women.

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:45.550
And it's out of that performance

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.710
that Rutt got together
with his collaborator,

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.850
a guy by the name of Underwood,

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.320
to come up with this marketing strategy

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.010
for the Pearl Milling Company's brand

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:00.063
of self-raising flour.

00:06:01.397 --> 00:06:03.020
In the 1920s,

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.400
Quaker Oats bought Pearl Milling Company,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.870
increasing the market
share and cultural presence

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:08.703
of the brand.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.150
Around the same time,

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.380
mob violence and new forms of repression

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.433
against African Americans
were becoming more widespread.

00:06:17.693 --> 00:06:20.560
In the small Missouri town
where the milling company

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.580
behind Aunt Jemima's was based,

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.080
a 19-year-old Black
man was lynched in 1933

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.903
after he was accused of making
a pass at a White woman.

00:06:30.219 --> 00:06:31.220
That's the immediate context,

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.880
because if you think about it,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.880
in comparison to the sort
of brutal lynching of

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:41.700
a young African American man,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.620
Aunt Jemima's something very different.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.503
It's a contrast to that sort
of threat of Black masculinity.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.840
Aunt Jemima is the embodiment of

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:57.560
the sort of safe, loyal,
trusting house servant

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:59.520
of slavery times.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.490
So that imagery of Aunt Jemima was

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:06.720
both reassuring and
nostalgic to White Americans,

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.780
and particularly White Southerners.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.637
Nostalgia is an important emotion.

00:07:11.637 --> 00:07:14.283
People buy products on emotion.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:17.273
For generations,

00:07:17.273 --> 00:07:19.190
frosty mornings have seemed warmer

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:20.180
with stacks of...

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:21.430
Aunt Jemima Buckwheats.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:25.380
Quaker tweaked the brand image over time,

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.430
replacing the kerchief on the
Aunt Jemima character's head

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:31.270
with a plaid hairband in 1968.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:34.540
And again, in 1989, by
adding pearl earrings,

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:35.373
a lace collar

00:07:35.373 --> 00:07:37.240
and then-fashionable perm hairstyle.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.390
It's got more maple in it.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.979
In the early '90s,

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:43.310
a commercial campaign starring
performer Gladys Knight

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:45.720
tried to further normalize Aunt Jemima

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.410
by showing how much African
Americans themselves loved

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:49.920
the product.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.710
But even after the brand was acquired

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:53.760
by PepsiCo in 2001,

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.210
some still felt that the
minor branding shifts

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.163
over the years were mostly a convenient

00:07:58.163 --> 00:08:02.060
corporate distraction from
a lingering racial bias.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.810
Aunt Jemima was a very strong brand,

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.000
strong enough where it
was never discontinued.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.520
And when we look at these products,

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.060
the ethos of them

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.710
and just being easy, ready-made products,

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.750
that was the whole design.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.790
To bring in these caricatures,

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.780
to convey this ease

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.200
as if you have this person, "Mammy,"

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.470
in your kitchen, cooking
the pancakes for you.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:25.860
It's that easy that

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.330
it's as if someone else did it for you.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:28.930
So racism is commerce,

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:31.010
and in terms of this marketing,

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.520
unfortunately it was
hinged on a lot of this.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.700
Aunt Jemima buttermilk pancakes.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.058
Perfect pancakes in 10 shakes.

00:08:38.058 --> 00:08:40.130
This is a classic example

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:42.626
of a corporation commercializing

00:08:42.626 --> 00:08:47.010
and capitalizing on racial stereotypes,

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.530
and making in the process

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.873
billions and billions
of dollars in profit.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:54.850
Aunt Jemima is history.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:57.360
Quaker Oats announcing
today that it is retiring

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.460
both the logo and the name

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.683
of the 130-year-old brand.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.160
Quaker, the latest company

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.350
to take action as
demonstrations protesting

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.720
racial inequality continue
across the country.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.780
PepsiCo has recognized

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.890
that we are in one of those moments,

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:16.600
and that's to not lose market share,

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.760
and to seem as though it's
continuing to be relevant,

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.130
they need to make this attempt.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:23.190
And so, they've done that with

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:26.104
the Pearl Milling Company branding.

00:09:26.104 --> 00:09:29.830
Now the Pearl Milling Company
branding sort of harken backs

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:33.520
to the very beginning of
this in St. Joseph, Missouri,

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:35.970
that era in relation to racial violence.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.680
I don't know that it goes far enough

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.690
to separate it from that racist

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:43.510
and oppressive past

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:48.363
that so many African American
people were exposed to.

00:10:04.281 --> 00:10:06.370
While Aunt Jemima was probably

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.210
the most notorious mainstream brand

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:11.340
to face a reckoning
after the 2020 protests,

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.960
a few others, such as Land
O'Lakes and Uncle Ben's,

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:15.530
have also come under scrutiny

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:17.520
in the wake of the renewed conversations

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.779
around systematic racism.

00:10:19.779 --> 00:10:22.850
As corporations continue to navigate

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.750
how to address racial
stereotypes in advertising

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.800
while still protecting profits,

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.570
there's a whole other arena now

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.160
where the problem of
embedded racism may be

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.880
even harder to detect

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.603
and harder to stamp out.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:39.340
I think my blackness is interfering

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:42.260
with the computer's ability to follow me.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:44.590
As you can see, I do this,

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:46.270
no following.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:47.430
Not really,

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:48.690
not really following me.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:50.090
I back up,

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:51.130
I get really, really close

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:52.890
to try to let the camera recognize me,

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:53.970
not happening.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.180
Now, my White coworker, Wanda,

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:57.450
is about to slide in the frame,

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:00.880
you'll immediately see
what I'm talking about.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.966
Wanda, if you would, please.

00:11:02.966 --> 00:11:03.799
Sure.

00:11:08.633 --> 00:11:09.466
It stems from the roots,

00:11:09.466 --> 00:11:12.200
and AI is just the next branch.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:14.210
Everything is the exact same

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:15.720
when it comes to the application,

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.040
the technology behind it.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.974
Because when you look
at who's in the room,

00:11:18.974 --> 00:11:21.860
it's not Black people who are testing

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.560
these AI algorithms.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:25.720
The system I was using worked well

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:27.614
on my lighter skin friend's face,

00:11:27.614 --> 00:11:29.992
but when it came to detecting my face,

00:11:29.992 --> 00:11:32.360
it didn't do so well,

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.353
until I put on a white mask.

00:11:34.353 --> 00:11:37.580
There's a really groundbreaking piece

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:40.318
of research that I think
many people sort of associate

00:11:40.318 --> 00:11:42.920
with this concern and movement

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:45.460
around AI fairness and bias,

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:47.640
and that's the 2018 research done

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.600
by Joy Buolamwini and Timnit Gebru.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.070
They were trying to figure out

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:54.900
whether popular
facial-recognition programs could

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:58.930
correctly pick out the gender
of different photographs.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:11:59.930
And what they found was that

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:02.500
the facial-recognition
software that they looked at,

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.550
which was from Microsoft, IBM, and Face++,

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:10.360
misidentified darker-skinned
women far more frequently.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.903
And a few months later,

00:12:11.903 --> 00:12:14.796
Buolamwini along with Deborah Raji,

00:12:14.796 --> 00:12:16.750
published a subsequent paper that looked

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.150
at Amazon's cloud
facial-recognition program,

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:20.650
which is called Rekognition.

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:27.860
They were already pitching
it for use by police

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:30.280
and by immigration services,

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:31.257
and we pretty much said,

00:12:31.257 --> 00:12:33.247
"Well, these products are
already out there in the market.

00:12:33.247 --> 00:12:34.777
Do they actually work for faces

00:12:34.777 --> 00:12:36.067
that look like ours?

00:12:36.067 --> 00:12:37.627
Darker, female faces."

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.812
What we found was that
these models were performing

00:12:44.812 --> 00:12:46.840
at less than 70% accuracy

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:48.330
for darker female faces,

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:50.820
while performing at
pretty much 100% accuracy

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:52.290
for lighter male faces,

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.420
revealing a discrepancy in the performance

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.410
of these systems for
different demographic groups,

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:59.671
and in particular, sort of
endangering the lives of

00:12:59.671 --> 00:13:01.910
those that would be impacted

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:03.660
by the deployment of these systems.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:06.370
Eventually Microsoft fixed

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.400
its facial-recognition software,

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:09.550
and big tech companies,

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.600
including Amazon and IBM,

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.240
said they would stop selling
facial-recognition products

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:15.860
to the police.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:18.950
Some local governments have
also passed laws regulating

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:20.850
facial-recognition software,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.860
or banning its use in policing,

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:26.260
but there's no federal
law regulating its use.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:28.170
At the same time, there
are other companies

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:30.110
that sell their
facial-recognition software

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:30.943
to police departments.

00:13:30.943 --> 00:13:32.600
And there've been at least three cases

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.430
of Black men who are suing

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:36.900
because they say that
they were wrongly flagged

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:38.770
by this facial-recognition software.

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:40.400
Police came after them because

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:43.190
the software told them
that they were a suspect

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:44.550
they were looking for.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.850
This is what happened in
the case of Robert Williams,

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:49.410
who was one of the first recorded cases

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.550
of a false facial-recognition
match escalating

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:54.100
to a false arrest.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:55.850
I picked that paper up

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:57.560
and hold it next to my face.

00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:59.960
And I said, "This is not me."

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:01.607
I was like, "I hope you all don't think

00:14:01.607 --> 00:14:03.460
all Black people look alike."

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:06.380
And then he says, "The
computer says it's you."

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:07.870
Facial recognition is not

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:10.950
the only example of algorithmic bias.

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.150
Machine learning, a subset of AI,

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:14.930
powers many technologies.

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:18.040
Huge data sets are used to
train machine-learning models

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:19.758
to make predictions.

00:14:19.758 --> 00:14:22.680
So we want a computer, for example,

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:25.890
to learn the difference
between a dog and a cat.

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:28.060
We can show the computer a lot of examples

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:29.380
of what a dog looks like,

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:30.950
show the computer a lot of examples of

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:32.390
what a cat looks like.

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:34.110
And as a result of that,

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:35.817
the computer kind of figures out that,

00:14:35.817 --> 00:14:38.067
well, pointy ears means a cat.

00:14:38.067 --> 00:14:39.517
Whiskers might mean a cat.

00:14:39.517 --> 00:14:41.490
Floppy ears means a dog,

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:44.410
and uses these features
in order to differentiate

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:47.180
between any new image that it might see

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:48.270
of what could potentially be

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:49.340
a dog or a cat.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:51.260
If all of your cats are white

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:54.530
and all of your dogs are gray and black,

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:55.980
what happens if you put in a photo

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:57.390
of a black cat?

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:58.440
All of the cats

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.440
that this algorithm has learned are cats

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:01.360
are white.

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:03.910
Everything that it's
seen is black is a dog.

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:05.827
And so, the algorithm may decide,

00:15:05.827 --> 00:15:08.050
oh, this is black, it must be a dog.

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:09.703
In reality, it's a black cat.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:12.780
Sometimes a data set

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:14.870
that lacks diversity is the problem.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:17.830
Other times, the design
of the model is the issue.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:20.620
It can be a completely
unaccountable process.

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:22.180
These data sets are so large.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:26.210
The AI system is analyzing data

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:27.910
that they haven't,

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.780
they can't even know the full scope of.

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:31.560
It's sort of on the order of millions

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:33.780
and millions of examples.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:36.650
The algorithm is sort
of identifying patterns

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:38.440
in order to create a relationship

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:40.022
between inputs and outputs,

00:15:40.022 --> 00:15:43.180
but those patterns are
not necessarily things

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:46.740
that humans can understand
or see very well.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:48.160
These algorithms should be able

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:49.340
to learn over time,

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:51.761
they should be able to
incorporate new data

00:15:51.761 --> 00:15:53.460
from the people using it.

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:55.360
Even if you create an algorithm,

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:57.860
you check it for bias and it's fine,

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:01.250
the new data, the way
people use the algorithm,

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:03.520
could work the algorithm over time

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:05.130
and make it biased.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:07.433
You know as an example
of what could go wrong,

00:16:07.433 --> 00:16:09.880
a ProPublica article looked at software

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.287
that's used to predict future criminals.

00:16:12.287 --> 00:16:16.180
Who is more likely or less
likely to commit a crime?

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:19.970
And a ProPublica investigation
found that that algorithm,

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:21.570
which was pretty widely used,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:23.900
was biased against Black people.

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:27.160
And the issue here is that
that algorithm gets used

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:28.360
for all sorts of things.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:32.920
It gets used for deciding
who gets what sort of bail,

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.560
who gets what sort of sentence,

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:36.060
who gets parole.

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:41.060
And the algorithm was
considering Black defendants

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:43.970
as a greater risk than White defendants.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:46.410
And ProPublica tracked
some of these cases,

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:47.870
and it actually turned out that

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:50.470
the White defendant did re-offend

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:52.016
and the Black defendant didn't.

00:16:52.016 --> 00:16:54.330
The unwillingness on the part

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.480
of some tech companies to fully disclose

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.718
their algorithms makes it
hard to hold them accountable.

00:16:59.718 --> 00:17:03.400
In April, the U.S. Federal
Trade Commission warned

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:05.010
that it could crack down on companies

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:07.150
that use biased AI software.

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.203
But overall, regulation has been slow.

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:14.150
So we're only going to
see these AI systems being

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:15.690
more and more prevalent,

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:20.080
invisibly ubiquitous in society.

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:23.010
If we don't do a good job
of really thinking about

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.030
how we're going to hold
them accountable now,

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:27.280
it's going to be even much
more difficult later on

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:30.010
to try to reel things back.

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:31.630
Just as with the ongoing fight

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:33.630
to create more equitable political

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.790
and socioeconomic systems,

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:38.750
overcoming racism in
product design may require

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:40.330
more than simply renaming,

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:42.417
rebranding or recoding.

00:17:42.417 --> 00:17:45.080
These changes, although helpful,

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.010
only scratch the surface
of the deeper problem.

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:50.870
It just says a lot about corporate America

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:55.180
when Black people ask for
equality for hundreds of years,

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:56.450
and then they're heard

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:59.377
in a span of three months last year,

00:17:59.377 --> 00:18:04.377
and the solution was
changing a box of pancakes.

